To: Data Protection Officer / Legal Counsel, MIRA
From: Elza Paegle, AI Compliance Consultant
Date: 18 August 2026
Subject: First-pass GDPR assessment of the MIRA MVP

Bottom line — proceed with conditions. MIRA may continue as a controlled prototype, but it should not be opened to real public users until its cloud/AI supply chain, sensitive-data controls, retention, and rights workflows are documented and technically enforced.

MIRA processes more than questionnaire data. Uploaded photographs, free text, contact details, Creative DNA and personality/style inferences, generated images, and workflow logs are personal data when linked to an identifiable user. Images or text may also reveal Article 9 characteristics or information about third parties, although MIRA does not need these data. The service scores users and uses innovative generative AI; those two DPIA criteria are enough to justify completing a DPIA before deployment. Article 22 is unlikely to apply because MIRA makes creative recommendations without legal or similarly significant effects and the user or photographer may reject every output.

The top three actions are:

Minimise and define the processing. Publish a layered privacy notice; make name/email optional where possible; prohibit third-party and special-category submissions; strip EXIF metadata; define an adults-only scope; and adopt automated limits of seven days for raw images and 30 days for other prototype inputs and outputs.

Contract and map every vendor before data flows. Execute Article 28 DPAs with Tally, n8n, Google, Railway, the LLM provider, and the image provider; record subprocessors and processing regions; disable provider training and secondary use; and document an EU–US Data Privacy Framework or SCC-based transfer mechanism, with a transfer assessment where required.

Complete the DPIA and implement controls. Build one user/submission identifier across systems, cascading erasure, access/export and correction workflows, role-based access, incident response, short log retention, consent records for optional research/cookies, and clear AI/synthetic-image disclosures.

Residual risks remain even after these measures: AI profiles may be inaccurate or reductive; users may upload images containing people who did not agree to processing; and vendor subprocessors, remote access, or model behaviour may change. Human review, recurring vendor checks, and output correction must therefore remain part of operations.

This memo is a preliminary compliance assessment—not a legal opinion, DPIA, certification, or guarantee of compliance. MIRA must engage qualified legal counsel before relying on it for deployment decisions.

